{"id":"n-20261026-graelc","category":"health","display_title":"The Truth Behind Ferritin Face","display_title_he":"האמת מאחורי פני פריטין","short_summary":"Social media users claim low iron levels leave visible clues on the face, but doctors warn that blood tests are essential for proper diagnosis.","english_level":"C1","words":470,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"BBC News","domain":"bbc.co.uk","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmlykvpdx4x0o","title":"Is 'ferritin face' a real thing - and can you really spot low iron from looks?","published_at":"2026-10-26T01:43:55.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-26T01:43:55.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-26T06:20:39.966Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":9,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["A viral health trend spreading across platforms like TikTok and Instagram has popularized the term \"ferritin face.\" Content creators point to physical features such as washed-out skin, dark under-eye circles, brittle hair, and weak nails, attributing these traits entirely to low iron levels. Ferritin is a crucial protein responsible for storing iron within the body. While millions of views have propelled this concept into the mainstream, medical professionals emphasize that external appearance alone cannot serve as a reliable medical diagnosis for iron deficiency.","According to general practitioners and haematologists, visible signs like pale skin or hair shedding can sometimes accompany low iron, but they are entirely non-specific and can indicate various other health conditions. Furthermore, experts note that visual assessments are especially unreliable for individuals with darker skin tones, as colour changes are less pronounced. Consultant haematologist Dr Drew Provan noted that it is impossible to determine someone's ferritin levels simply from how their face looks. Doctors explain that there is a significant physiological disconnect between iron stores and circulating haemoglobin, which is the protein in red blood cells responsible for carrying oxygen. It is entirely possible for an individual to have depleted iron stores while maintaining normal haemoglobin levels, meaning they experience symptoms without necessarily being anaemic. British Society of Gastroenterology guidelines state that ferritin below 15 micrograms per litre indicates absent iron stores, while levels below 30 generally indicate low iron stores.","While medical experts caution against self-diagnosis, many acknowledge that the social media discussion has empowered numerous women to take their symptoms more seriously and consult healthcare providers. Content creator Lucy, who posts online about her own health experiences, noted that social media helped her and other women ask questions when something felt wrong, though she is not a doctor. General practitioner Dr Aziza Sesay described social media as a double-edged sword that can encourage people to seek help while also leading to incorrect self-diagnosis. Doctors stress that blood tests are absolutely vital not only to confirm iron deficiency accurately, but also to uncover the root cause behind it. Underlying triggers can range from heavy menstrual periods and low dietary intake to gastrointestinal blood loss or absorption issues.","Medical guidelines suggest that treating iron deficiency appropriately often involves oral iron supplements and dietary adjustments, such as incorporating meat, beans, lentils, and leafy green vegetables. Consultant haematologist Dr Sue Pavord advised that iron tablets are best taken on an empty stomach first thing in the morning, while avoiding tea or coffee around that time because they inhibit iron absorption. However, specialists warn against taking iron supplements without professional guidance, as self-treating can mask underlying problems and potentially cause harm. Ultimately, healthcare professionals describe social media as a mixed phenomenon: while it encourages individuals to seek professional help, relying on brief online videos for medical diagnosis remains unreliable and potentially misleading."],"facts":[{"fact":"A viral social media trend termed 'ferritin face' claims that low iron stores leave visible clues on the face, such as pale skin, dark circles, and brittle hair.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Medical professionals state that appearance alone cannot diagnose iron deficiency, though visual signs can occasionally act as clues.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Ferritin measures stored iron, whereas haemoglobin carries oxygen in red blood cells; iron stores can become depleted before haemoglobin drops.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Doctors emphasize that proper diagnosis requires blood tests to confirm iron deficiency and determine its underlying cause.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Treatment for iron deficiency often involves oral iron supplements and dietary changes, though self-treating without medical advice can be harmful.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"viral","lemma":"viral","pos":"adj","he":"ויראלי","name":false},{"text":"health","lemma":"health","pos":"noun","he":"בריאות","name":false},{"text":"trend","lemma":"trend","pos":"noun","he":"מגמה","name":false},{"text":"spreading","lemma":"spread","pos":"verb","he":"מתפשט","name":false},{"text":"across","lemma":"across","pos":"prep","he":"ברחבי","name":false},{"text":"platforms","lemma":"platform","pos":"noun","he":"פלטפורמות","name":false},{"text":"like","lemma":"like","pos":"prep","he":"כמו","name":false},{"text":"popularized","lemma":"popularize","pos":"verb","he":"הפוך לפופולרי","name":false},{"text":"term","lemma":"term","pos":"noun","he":"מונח","name":false},{"text":"ferritin","lemma":"ferritin","pos":"noun","he":"פריטין","name":false},{"text":"face","lemma":"face","pos":"noun","he":"פנים","name":false},{"text":"content","lemma":"content","pos":"noun","he":"תוכן","name":false},{"text":"creators","lemma":"creator","pos":"noun","he":"יוצרים","name":false},{"text":"point"